    static let htmlTags: [String] = [
        "html\u{1}The html element represents the root of an HTML document.\u{1}manifest\u{3}Specifies the URI of a resource manifest indicating resources that should be cached locally. See Using the application cache for details.\u{3}\u{2}version\u{3}Specifies the version of the HTML Document Type Definition that governs the current document. This attribute is not needed, because it is redundant with the version information in the document type...\u{3}\u{2}xmlns\u{3}Specifies the XML Namespace of the document. Default value is \"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\". This is required in documents parsed with XML parsers, and optional in text/html documents.\u{3}",
        "head\u{1}The head element represents a collection of metadata for the Document.\u{1}profile\u{3}The URIs of one or more metadata profiles, separated by white space.\u{3}",
        "title\u{1}The title element represents the document's title or name. Authors should use titles that identify their documents even when they are used out of context, for example in a user's history or bookmarks, or in search results.\u{1}",
        "base\u{1}The base element allows authors to specify the document base URL for the purposes of resolving relative URLs, and the name of the default browsing context for the purposes of following hyperlinks.\u{1}href\u{3}The base URL to be used throughout the document for relative URL addresses. If this attribute is specified, this element must come before any other elements with attributes whose values are URLs.\u{3}\u{2}target\u{3}A name or keyword indicating the default location to display the result when hyperlinks or forms cause navigation, for elements that do not have an explicit target reference.\u{3}_self _blank _parent _top",
        "link\u{1}The link element allows authors to link their document to other resources.\u{1}href\u{3}This attribute specifies the URL is a text string specifying where a resource can be found on the Internet.\") of the linked resource. A URL can be absolute or relative.\u{3}\u{2}crossorigin\u{3}This enumerated attribute indicates whether CORS is a system, consisting of transmitting HTTP headers, that determines whether browsers block frontend JavaScript code from accessing responses for...\u{3}anonymous use-credentials\u{2}rel\u{3}This attribute names a relationship of the linked document to the current document. The attribute must be a space-separated list of the link types values.\u{3}\u{2}media\u{3}This attribute specifies the media that the linked resource applies to. Its value must be a media type / media query.\u{3}\u{2}hreflang\u{3}This attribute indicates the language of the linked resource. It is purely advisory. Allowed values are determined by BCP47. Use this attribute only if the href attribute is present.\u{3}\u{2}type\u{3}This attribute is used to define the type of the content linked to. The value of the attribute should be a MIME type such as text/html, text/css, and so on.\u{3}\u{2}sizes\u{3}This attribute defines the sizes of the icons for visual media contained in the resource. It must be present only if the rel contains a value of icon or a non-standard type such as Apple's...\u{3}\u{2}as\u{3}This attribute is only used when rel=\"preload\" or rel=\"prefetch\" has been set on the <link> element. It specifies the type of content being loaded by the <link>, which is necessary for content...\u{3}\u{2}importance\u{3}Indicates the relative importance of the resource. Priority hints are delegated using the values:\u{3}\u{2}importance\u{3}auto: Indicates no preference. The browser may use its own heuristics to decide the priority of the resource. high: Indicates to the browser that the resource is of high priority.\u{3}\u{2}integrity\u{3}Contains inline metadata \u{2014} a base64-encoded cryptographic hash of the resource (file) you\u{2019}re telling the browser to fetch.\u{3}\u{2}referrerpolicy\u{3}A string indicating which referrer to use when fetching the resource: no-referrer means that the Referer header will not be sent.\u{3}\u{2}title\u{3}The title attribute has special semantics on the <link> element. When used on a <link rel=\"stylesheet\"> it defines a preferred or an alternate stylesheet.\u{3}",
        "meta\u{1}The meta element represents various kinds of metadata that cannot be expressed using the title, base, link, style, and script elements.\u{1}name\u{3}This attribute defines the name of a piece of document-level metadata. It should not be set if one of the attributes itemprop, http-equiv or charset is also set.\u{3}\u{2}http-equiv\u{3}Defines a pragma directive. The attribute is named http-equiv(alent) because all the allowed values are names of particular HTTP headers: \"content-language\" Defines the default language of the page.\u{3}\u{2}content\u{3}This attribute contains the value for the http-equiv or name attribute, depending on which is used.\u{3}\u{2}charset\u{3}This attribute declares the page's character encoding. It must contain a standard IANA MIME name for character encodings.\u{3}\u{2}scheme\u{3}This attribute defines the scheme in which metadata is described. A scheme is a context leading to the correct interpretations of the content value, like a format.\u{3}",
        "style\u{1}The style element allows authors to embed style information in their documents. The style element is one of several inputs to the styling processing model. The element does not represent content for the user.\u{1}media\u{3}This attribute defines which media the style should be applied to. Its value is a media query, which defaults to all if the attribute is missing.\u{3}\u{2}nonce\u{3}A cryptographic nonce (number used once) used to allow inline styles in a style-src Content-Security-Policy. The server must generate a unique nonce value each time it transmits a policy.\u{3}\u{2}type\u{3}This attribute defines the styling language as a MIME type (charset should not be specified). This attribute is optional and defaults to text/css if it is not specified \u{2014} there is very little reason...\u{3}\u{2}scoped\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}title\u{3}This attribute specifies alternative style sheet sets.\u{3}",
        "body\u{1}The body element represents the content of the document.\u{1}onafterprint\u{3}Function to call after the user has printed the document.\u{3}\u{2}onbeforeprint\u{3}Function to call when the user requests printing of the document.\u{3}\u{2}onbeforeunload\u{3}Function to call when the document is about to be unloaded.\u{3}\u{2}onhashchange\u{3}Function to call when the fragment identifier part (starting with the hash ('#') character) of the document's current address has changed.\u{3}\u{2}onlanguagechange\u{3}Function to call when the preferred languages changed.\u{3}\u{2}onmessage\u{3}Function to call when the document has received a message.\u{3}\u{2}onoffline\u{3}Function to call when network communication has failed.\u{3}\u{2}ononline\u{3}Function to call when network communication has been restored.\u{3}\u{2}onpagehide\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}onpageshow\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}onpopstate\u{3}Function to call when the user has navigated session history.\u{3}\u{2}onstorage\u{3}Function to call when the storage area has changed.\u{3}\u{2}onunload\u{3}Function to call when the document is going away.\u{3}\u{2}alink\u{3}Color of text for hyperlinks when selected. This method is non-conforming, use CSS color property in conjunction with the :active that is being activated by the user.\") pseudo-class instead.\u{3}\u{2}background\u{3}URI of a image to use as a background. This method is non-conforming, use CSS background property on the element instead.\u{3}\u{2}bgcolor\u{3}Background color for the document. This method is non-conforming, use CSS background-color property on the element instead.\u{3}\u{2}bottommargin\u{3}The margin of the bottom of the body. This method is non-conforming, use CSS margin-bottom property on the element instead.\u{3}\u{2}leftmargin\u{3}The margin of the left of the body. This method is non-conforming, use CSS margin-left property on the element instead.\u{3}\u{2}link\u{3}Color of text for unvisited hypertext links. This method is non-conforming, use CSS color property in conjunction with the :link pseudo-class instead.\u{3}\u{2}onblur\u{3}Function to call when the document loses focus.\u{3}\u{2}onerror\u{3}Function to call when the document fails to load properly.\u{3}\u{2}onfocus\u{3}Function to call when the document receives focus.\u{3}\u{2}onload\u{3}Function to call when the document has finished loading.\u{3}\u{2}onredo\u{3}Function to call when the user has moved forward in undo transaction history.\u{3}\u{2}onresize\u{3}Function to call when the document has been resized.\u{3}\u{2}onundo\u{3}Function to call when the user has moved backward in undo transaction history.\u{3}\u{2}rightmargin\u{3}The margin of the right of the body. This method is non-conforming, use CSS margin-right property on the element instead.\u{3}\u{2}text\u{3}Foreground color of text. This method is non-conforming, use CSS color property on the element instead.\u{3}\u{2}topmargin\u{3}The margin of the top of the body. This method is non-conforming, use CSS margin-top property on the element instead.\u{3}\u{2}vlink\u{3}Color of text for visited hypertext links. This method is non-conforming, use CSS color property in conjunction with the :visited pseudo-class instead.\u{3}",
