        "pre\u{1}The pre element represents a block of preformatted text, in which structure is represented by typographic conventions rather than by elements.\u{1}cols\u{3}Contains the preferred count of characters that a line should have. It was a non-standard synonym of width. To achieve such an effect, use CSS width instead.\u{3}\u{2}width\u{3}Contains the preferred count of characters that a line should have. Though technically still implemented, this attribute has no visual effect; to achieve such an effect, use CSS width instead.\u{3}\u{2}wrap\u{3}Is a hint indicating how the overflow must happen. In modern browser this hint is ignored and no visual effect results in its present; to achieve such an effect, use CSS white-space instead.\u{3}",
        "blockquote\u{1}The blockquote element represents content that is quoted from another source, optionally with a citation which must be within a footer or cite element, and optionally with in-line changes such as annotations and abbreviations.\u{1}cite\u{3}A URL that designates a source document or message for the information quoted. This attribute is intended to point to information explaining the context or the reference for the quote.\u{3}",
        "ol\u{1}The ol element represents a list of items, where the items have been intentionally ordered, such that changing the order would change the meaning of the document.\u{1}reversed\u{3}This Boolean attribute specifies that the items of the list are specified in reversed order.\u{3}\u{2}start\u{3}This integer attribute specifies the start value for numbering the individual list items. Although the ordering type of list elements might be Roman numerals, such as XXXI, or letters, the value of...\u{3}\u{2}type\u{3}Indicates the numbering type: 'a' indicates lowercase letters, 'A' indicates uppercase letters, 'i' indicates lowercase Roman numerals, 'I' indicates uppercase Roman numerals, and '1' indicates...\u{3}1 a A i I\u{2}compact\u{3}This Boolean attribute hints that the list should be rendered in a compact style. The interpretation of this attribute depends on the user agent and it doesn't work in all browsers.\u{3}",
        "ul\u{1}The ul element represents a list of items, where the order of the items is not important \u{2014} that is, where changing the order would not materially change the meaning of the document.\u{1}compact\u{3}This Boolean attribute hints that the list should be rendered in a compact style. The interpretation of this attribute depends on the user agent and it doesn't work in all browsers.\u{3}",
        "li\u{1}The li element represents a list item. If its parent element is an ol, ul, or menu element, then the element is an item of the parent element's list, as defined for those elements.\u{1}value\u{3}This integer attribute indicates the current ordinal value of the list item as defined by the <ol> element.\u{3}\u{2}type\u{3}This character attribute indicates the numbering type: a: lowercase letters A: uppercase letters i: lowercase Roman numerals I: uppercase Roman numerals 1: numbers This type overrides the one used by...\u{3}",
        "dl\u{1}The dl element represents an association list consisting of zero or more name-value groups (a description list). A name-value group consists of one or more names (dt elements) followed by one or more values (dd elements),...\u{1}",
        "dt\u{1}The dt element represents the term, or name, part of a term-description group in a description list (dl element).\u{1}",
        "dd\u{1}The dd element represents the description, definition, or value, part of a term-description group in a description list (dl element).\u{1}nowrap\u{3}If the value of this attribute is set to yes, the definition text will not wrap. The default value is no.\u{3}",
        "figure\u{1}The figure element represents some flow content, optionally with a caption, that is self-contained (like a complete sentence) and is typically referenced as a single unit from the main flow of the document.\u{1}",
        "figcaption\u{1}The figcaption element represents a caption or legend for the rest of the contents of the figcaption element's parent figure element, if any.\u{1}",
        "main\u{1}The main element represents the main content of the body of a document or application. The main content area consists of content that is directly related to or expands upon the central topic of a document or central functionality...\u{1}",
        "div\u{1}The div element has no special meaning at all. It represents its children. It can be used with the class, lang, and title attributes to mark up semantics common to a group of consecutive elements.\u{1}",
        "a\u{1}If the a element has an href attribute, then it represents a hyperlink (a hypertext anchor) labeled by its contents.\u{1}href\u{3}Contains a URL or a URL fragment that the hyperlink points to. A URL fragment is a name preceded by a hash mark (#), which specifies an internal target location (an id of an HTML element) within the...\u{3}\u{2}target\u{3}Specifies where to display the linked URL. It is a name of, or keyword for, a browsing context: a tab, window, or <iframe>.\u{3}_self _blank _parent _top\u{2}download\u{3}This attribute instructs browsers to download a URL instead of navigating to it, so the user will be prompted to save it as a local file.\u{3}\u{2}ping\u{3}Contains a space-separated list of URLs to which, when the hyperlink is followed, POST requests with the body PING will be sent by the browser (in the background). Typically used for tracking.\u{3}\u{2}rel\u{3}Specifies the relationship of the target object to the link object. The value is a space-separated list of link types.\u{3}\u{2}hreflang\u{3}This attribute indicates the human language of the linked resource. It is purely advisory, with no built-in functionality. Allowed values are determined by BCP47.\u{3}\u{2}type\u{3}Specifies the media type in the form of a MIME type is a string sent along with a file indicating the type of the file (describing the content format, for example, a sound file might be labeled...\u{3}\u{2}referrerpolicy\u{3}Indicates which referrer to send when fetching the URL: 'no-referrer' means the Referer: header will not be sent.\u{3}",
        "em\u{1}The em element represents stress emphasis of its contents.\u{1}",
        "strong\u{1}The strong element represents strong importance, seriousness, or urgency for its contents.\u{1}",
        "small\u{1}The small element represents side comments such as small print.\u{1}",
        "s\u{1}The s element represents contents that are no longer accurate or no longer relevant.\u{1}",
        "cite\u{1}The cite element represents a reference to a creative work. It must include the title of the work or the name of the author(person, people or organization) or an URL reference, or a reference in abbreviated form as per the...\u{1}",
        "q\u{1}The q element represents some phrasing content quoted from another source.\u{1}cite\u{3}The value of this attribute is a URL that designates a source document or message for the information quoted.\u{3}",
        "dfn\u{1}The dfn element represents the defining instance of a term. The paragraph, description list group, or section that is the nearest ancestor of the dfn element must also contain the definition(s) for the term given by the dfn...\u{1}",
        "abbr\u{1}The abbr element represents an abbreviation or acronym, optionally with its expansion. The title attribute may be used to provide an expansion of the abbreviation.\u{1}",
        "ruby\u{1}The ruby element allows one or more spans of phrasing content to be marked with ruby annotations. Ruby annotations are short runs of text presented alongside base text, primarily used in East Asian typography as a guide for...\u{1}",
        "rb\u{1}The rb element marks the base text component of a ruby annotation. When it is the child of a ruby element, it doesn't represent anything itself, but its parent ruby element uses it as part of determining what it represents.\u{1}",
        "rt\u{1}The rt element marks the ruby text component of a ruby annotation. When it is the child of a ruby element or of an rtc element that is itself the child of a ruby element, it doesn't represent anything itself, but its ancestor...\u{1}",
        "rp\u{1}The rp element is used to provide fallback text to be shown by user agents that don't support ruby annotations. One widespread convention is to provide parentheses around the ruby text component of a ruby annotation.\u{1}",
        "time\u{1}The time element represents its contents, along with a machine-readable form of those contents in the datetime attribute.\u{1}datetime\u{3}This attribute indicates the time and/or date of the element and must be in one of the formats described below.\u{3}",
        "code\u{1}The code element represents a fragment of computer code. This could be an XML element name, a file name, a computer program, or any other string that a computer would recognize.\u{1}",
        "var\u{1}The var element represents a variable. This could be an actual variable in a mathematical expression or programming context, an identifier representing a constant, a symbol identifying a physical quantity, a function parameter,...\u{1}",
        "samp\u{1}The samp element represents sample or quoted output from another program or computing system.\u{1}",
        "kbd\u{1}The kbd element represents user input (typically keyboard input, although it may also be used to represent other input, such as voice commands).\u{1}",
        "sub\u{1}The sub element represents a subscript.\u{1}",
        "sup\u{1}The sup element represents a superscript.\u{1}",
        "i\u{1}The i element represents a span of text in an alternate voice or mood, or otherwise offset from the normal prose in a manner indicating a different quality of text, such as a taxonomic designation, a technical term, an idiomatic...\u{1}",
        "b\u{1}The b element represents a span of text to which attention is being drawn for utilitarian purposes without conveying any extra importance and with no implication of an alternate voice or mood, such as key words in a document...\u{1}",
        "u\u{1}The u element represents a span of text with an unarticulated, though explicitly rendered, non-textual annotation, such as labeling the text as being a proper name in Chinese text (a Chinese proper name mark), or labeling the...\u{1}",
        "mark\u{1}The mark element represents a run of text in one document marked or highlighted for reference purposes, due to its relevance in another context.\u{1}",
        "bdi\u{1}The bdi element represents a span of text that is to be isolated from its surroundings for the purposes of bidirectional text formatting. [BIDI]\u{1}",
        "bdo\u{1}The bdo element represents explicit text directionality formatting control for its children. It allows authors to override the Unicode bidirectional algorithm by explicitly specifying a direction override. [BIDI]\u{1}dir\u{3}The direction in which text should be rendered in this element's contents. Possible values are: ltr: Indicates that the text should go in a left-to-right direction.\u{3}",
        "span\u{1}The span element doesn't mean anything on its own, but can be useful when used together with the global attributes, e.g. class, lang, or dir. It represents its children.\u{1}",
        "br\u{1}The br element represents a line break.\u{1}clear\u{3}Indicates where to begin the next line after the break.\u{3}",
        "wbr\u{1}The wbr element represents a line break opportunity.\u{1}",
        "ins\u{1}The ins element represents an addition to the document.\u{1}cite\u{3}This attribute defines the URI of a resource that explains the change, such as a link to meeting minutes or a ticket in a troubleshooting system.\u{3}\u{2}datetime\u{3}This attribute indicates the time and date of the change and must be a valid date with an optional time string.\u{3}",
        "del\u{1}The del element represents a removal from the document.\u{1}cite\u{3}A URI for a resource that explains the change (for example, meeting minutes).\u{3}\u{2}datetime\u{3}This attribute indicates the time and date of the change and must be a valid date string with an optional time.\u{3}",
        "picture\u{1}The picture element is a container which provides multiple sources to its contained img element to allow authors to declaratively control or give hints to the user agent about which image resource to use, based on the screen...\u{1}",
        "img\u{1}An img element represents an image.\u{1}alt\u{3}This attribute defines an alternative text description of the image. Note: Browsers do not always display the image referenced by the element.\u{3}\u{2}src\u{3}The image URL. This attribute is mandatory for the <img> element. On browsers supporting srcset, src is treated like a candidate image with a pixel density descriptor 1x unless an image with this...\u{3}\u{2}srcset\u{3}A list of one or more strings separated by commas indicating a set of possible image sources for the user agent to use. Each string is composed of: 1. a URL to an image, 2.\u{3}\u{2}crossorigin\u{3}This enumerated attribute indicates if the fetching of the related image must be done using CORS or not.\u{3}anonymous use-credentials\u{2}usemap\u{3}The partial URL (starting with '#') of an image map associated with the element. Note: You cannot use this attribute if the <img> element is a descendant of an <a> creates a hyperlink to other web...\u{3}\u{2}ismap\u{3}This Boolean attribute indicates that the image is part of a server-side map. If so, the precise coordinates of a click are sent to the server.\u{3}\u{2}width\u{3}The intrinsic width of the image in pixels.\u{3}\u{2}height\u{3}The intrinsic height of the image in pixels.\u{3}\u{2}decoding\u{3}Provides an image decoding hint to the browser. The allowed values are: sync Decode the image synchronously for atomic presentation with other content.\u{3}sync async auto\u{2}loading\u{3}Indicates how the browser should load the image.\u{3}eager lazy\u{2}fetchpriority\u{3}Provides a hint of the relative priority to use when fetching the image.\u{3}high low auto\u{2}referrerpolicy\u{3}A string indicating which referrer to use when fetching the resource: no-referrer: The Referer header will not be sent.\u{3}no-referrer no-referrer-when-downgrade origin origin-when-cross-origin same-origin strict-origin strict-origin-when-cross-origin unsafe-url\u{2}sizes\u{3}A list of one or more strings separated by commas indicating a set of source sizes. Each source size consists of: 1. a media condition. This must be omitted for the last item. 2. a source size value.\u{3}\u{2}importance\u{3}Indicates the relative importance of the resource. Priority hints are delegated using the values:\u{3}\u{2}importance\u{3}auto: Indicates no preference. The browser may use its own heuristics to decide the priority of the image. high: Indicates to the browser that the image is of high priority.\u{3}\u{2}intrinsicsize\u{3}This attribute tells the browser to ignore the actual intrinsic size of the image and pretend it\u{2019}s the size specified in the attribute.\u{3}",
        "iframe\u{1}The iframe element represents a nested browsing context.\u{1}src\u{3}The URL of the page to embed. Use a value of about:blank to embed an empty page that conforms to the same-origin policy. Also note that programatically removing an <iframe>'s src attribute (e.g.\u{3}\u{2}srcdoc\u{3}Inline HTML to embed, overriding the src attribute. If a browser does not support the srcdoc attribute, it will fall back to the URL in the src attribute.\u{3}\u{2}name\u{3}A targetable name for the embedded browsing context. This can be used in the target attribute of the <a> creates a hyperlink to other web pages, files, locations within the same page, email...\u{3}\u{2}sandbox\u{3}Applies extra restrictions to the content in the frame. The value of the attribute can either be empty to apply all restrictions, or space-separated tokens to lift particular restrictions:...\u{3}allow-forms allow-modals allow-pointer-lock allow-popups allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox allow-same-origin allow-scripts allow-top-navigation\u{2}seamless\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}allowfullscreen\u{3}Set to true if the <iframe> can activate fullscreen mode by calling the requestFullscreen() method issues an asynchronous request to make the element be displayed in full-screen mode.\") method.\u{3}\u{2}width\u{3}The width of the frame in CSS pixels. Default is 300.\u{3}\u{2}height\u{3}The height of the frame in CSS pixels. Default is 150.\u{3}\u{2}allow\u{3}Specifies a feature policy for the <iframe>.\u{3}\u{2}allowpaymentrequest\u{3}Set to true if a cross-origin <iframe> should be allowed to invoke the Payment Request API.\u{3}\u{2}allowpaymentrequest\u{3}This attribute is considered a legacy attribute and redefined as allow=\"payment\".\u{3}\u{2}csp\u{3}A Content Security Policy enforced for the embedded resource. See HTMLIFrameElement.csp for details.\u{3}\u{2}importance\u{3}The download priority of the resource in the <iframe>'s src attribute. Allowed values: auto (default) No preference. The browser uses its own heuristics to decide the priority of the resource.\u{3}\u{2}referrerpolicy\u{3}Indicates which referrer to send when fetching the frame's resource: no-referrer: The Referer header will not be sent.\u{3}",
        "embed\u{1}The embed element provides an integration point for an external (typically non-HTML) application or interactive content.\u{1}src\u{3}The URL of the resource being embedded.\u{3}\u{2}type\u{3}The MIME type to use to select the plug-in to instantiate.\u{3}\u{2}width\u{3}The displayed width of the resource, in CSS pixels. This must be an absolute value; percentages are not allowed.\u{3}\u{2}height\u{3}The displayed height of the resource, in CSS pixels. This must be an absolute value; percentages are not allowed.\u{3}",
        "object\u{1}The object element can represent an external resource, which, depending on the type of the resource, will either be treated as an image, as a nested browsing context, or as an external resource to be processed by a plugin.\u{1}data\u{3}The address of the resource as a valid URL. At least one of data and type must be defined.\u{3}\u{2}type\u{3}The content type of the resource specified by data. At least one of data and type must be defined.\u{3}\u{2}typemustmatch\u{3}This Boolean attribute indicates if the type attribute and the actual content type of the resource must match to be used.\u{3}\u{2}name\u{3}The name of valid browsing context (HTML5), or the name of the control (HTML 4).\u{3}\u{2}usemap\u{3}A hash-name reference to a <map>.\") element; that is a '#' followed by the value of a name of a map element.\u{3}\u{2}form\u{3}The form element, if any, that the object element is associated with (its form owner). The value of the attribute must be an ID of a <form> element in the same document.\u{3}\u{2}width\u{3}The width of the display resource, in CSS pixels. -- (Absolute values only. NO percentages)\u{3}\u{2}height\u{3}The height of the displayed resource, in CSS pixels. -- (Absolute values only. NO percentages)\u{3}\u{2}archive\u{3}A space-separated list of URIs for archives of resources for the object.\u{3}\u{2}border\u{3}The width of a border around the control, in pixels.\u{3}\u{2}classid\u{3}The URI of the object's implementation. It can be used together with, or in place of, the data attribute.\u{3}\u{2}codebase\u{3}The base path used to resolve relative URIs specified by classid, data, or archive. If not specified, the default is the base URI of the current document.\u{3}\u{2}codetype\u{3}The content type of the data specified by classid.\u{3}\u{2}declare\u{3}The presence of this Boolean attribute makes this element a declaration only. The object must be instantiated by a subsequent <object> element.\u{3}\u{2}standby\u{3}A message that the browser can show while loading the object's implementation and data.\u{3}\u{2}tabindex\u{3}The position of the element in the tabbing navigation order for the current document.\u{3}",
        "param\u{1}The param element defines parameters for plugins invoked by object elements. It does not represent anything on its own.\u{1}name\u{3}Name of the parameter.\u{3}\u{2}value\u{3}Specifies the value of the parameter.\u{3}\u{2}type\u{3}Only used if the valuetype is set to \"ref\". Specifies the MIME type of values found at the URI specified by value.\u{3}\u{2}valuetype\u{3}Specifies the type of the value attribute. Possible values are: data: Default value. The value is passed to the object's implementation as a string.\u{3}",
        "video\u{1}A video element is used for playing videos or movies, and audio files with captions.\u{1}src\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}crossorigin\u{3}\u{3}anonymous use-credentials\u{2}poster\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}preload\u{3}\u{3}none metadata auto\u{2}autoplay\u{3}A Boolean attribute; if specified, the video automatically begins to play back as soon as it can do so without stopping to finish loading the data.\u{3}\u{2}mediagroup\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}loop\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}muted\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}controls\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}width\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}height\u{3}\u{3}",
        "audio\u{1}An audio element represents a sound or audio stream.\u{1}src\u{3}The URL of the audio to embed. This is subject to HTTP access controls. This is optional; you may instead use the <source> element within the audio block to specify the audio to embed.\u{3}\u{2}crossorigin\u{3}This enumerated attribute indicates whether to use CORS to fetch the related image. CORS-enabled resources can be reused in the <canvas> element without being tainted.\u{3}anonymous use-credentials\u{2}preload\u{3}This enumerated attribute is intended to provide a hint to the browser about what the author thinks will lead to the best user experience.\u{3}none metadata auto\u{2}autoplay\u{3}A Boolean attribute: if specified, the audio will automatically begin playback as soon as it can do so, without waiting for the entire audio file to finish downloading.\u{3}\u{2}mediagroup\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}loop\u{3}A Boolean attribute: if specified, the audio player will automatically seek back to the start upon reaching the end of the audio.\u{3}\u{2}muted\u{3}A Boolean attribute that indicates whether the audio will be initially silenced. Its default value is false.\u{3}\u{2}controls\u{3}If this attribute is present, the browser will offer controls to allow the user to control audio playback, including volume, seeking, and pause/resume playback.\u{3}",
        "source\u{1}The source element allows authors to specify multiple alternative media resources for media elements. It does not represent anything on its own.\u{1}src\u{3}Required for <audio> and <video> embeds a media player which supports video playback into the document.\"), address of the media resource.\u{3}\u{2}type\u{3}The MIME-type of the resource, optionally with a codecs parameter. See RFC 4281 for information about how to specify codecs.\u{3}\u{2}sizes\u{3}Is a list of source sizes that describes the final rendered width of the image represented by the source. Each source size consists of a comma-separated list of media condition-length pairs.\u{3}\u{2}srcset\u{3}A list of one or more strings separated by commas indicating a set of possible images represented by the source for the browser to use. Each string is composed of: 1. one URL to an image, 2.\u{3}\u{2}media\u{3}Media query of the resource's intended media; this should be used only in a <picture> element.\u{3}",
        "track\u{1}The track element allows authors to specify explicit external timed text tracks for media elements. It does not represent anything on its own.\u{1}default\u{3}This attribute indicates that the track should be enabled unless the user's preferences indicate that another track is more appropriate. This may only be used on one track element per media element.\u{3}\u{2}kind\u{3}How the text track is meant to be used. If omitted the default kind is subtitles. If the attribute is not present, it will use the subtitles.\u{3}subtitles captions descriptions chapters metadata\u{2}label\u{3}A user-readable title of the text track which is used by the browser when listing available text tracks.\u{3}\u{2}src\u{3}Address of the track (.vtt file). Must be a valid URL. This attribute must be specified and its URL value must have the same origin as the document \u{2014} unless the <audio> or <video> embeds a media...\u{3}\u{2}srclang\u{3}Language of the track text data. It must be a valid BCP 47 language tag. If the kind attribute is set to subtitles, then srclang must be defined.\u{3}",
        "map\u{1}The map element, in conjunction with an img element and any area element descendants, defines an image map. The element represents its children.\u{1}name\u{3}The name attribute gives the map a name so that it can be referenced. The attribute must be present and must have a non-empty value with no space characters.\u{3}",
        "area\u{1}The area element represents either a hyperlink with some text and a corresponding area on an image map, or a dead area on an image map.\u{1}alt\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}coords\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}shape\u{3}\u{3}circle default poly rect\u{2}href\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}target\u{3}\u{3}_self _blank _parent _top\u{2}download\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}ping\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}rel\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}hreflang\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}type\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}accesskey\u{3}Specifies a keyboard navigation accelerator for the element. Pressing ALT or a similar key in association with the specified character selects the form control correlated with that key sequence.\u{3}",
        "table\u{1}The table element represents data with more than one dimension, in the form of a table.\u{1}border\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}align\u{3}This enumerated attribute indicates how the table must be aligned inside the containing document. It may have the following values: left: the table is displayed on the left side of the document;...\u{3}",
        "caption\u{1}The caption element represents the title of the table that is its parent, if it has a parent and that is a table element.\u{1}align\u{3}This enumerated attribute indicates how the caption must be aligned with respect to the table. It may have one of the following values: left The caption is displayed to the left of the table.\u{3}",
        "colgroup\u{1}The colgroup element represents a group of one or more columns in the table that is its parent, if it has a parent and that is a table element.\u{1}span\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}align\u{3}This enumerated attribute specifies how horizontal alignment of each column cell content will be handled.\u{3}",
        "col\u{1}If a col element has a parent and that is a colgroup element that itself has a parent that is a table element, then the col element represents one or more columns in the column group represented by that colgroup.\u{1}span\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}align\u{3}This enumerated attribute specifies how horizontal alignment of each column cell content will be handled.\u{3}",
        "tbody\u{1}The tbody element represents a block of rows that consist of a body of data for the parent table element, if the tbody element has a parent and it is a table.\u{1}align\u{3}This enumerated attribute specifies how horizontal alignment of each cell content will be handled. Possible values are: left, aligning the content to the left of the cell center, centering the...\u{3}",
        "thead\u{1}The thead element represents the block of rows that consist of the column labels (headers) for the parent table element, if the thead element has a parent and it is a table.\u{1}align\u{3}This enumerated attribute specifies how horizontal alignment of each cell content will be handled. Possible values are: left, aligning the content to the left of the cell center, centering the...\u{3}",
        "tfoot\u{1}The tfoot element represents the block of rows that consist of the column summaries (footers) for the parent table element, if the tfoot element has a parent and it is a table.\u{1}align\u{3}This enumerated attribute specifies how horizontal alignment of each cell content will be handled. Possible values are: left, aligning the content to the left of the cell center, centering the...\u{3}",
        "tr\u{1}The tr element represents a row of cells in a table.\u{1}align\u{3}A DOMString which specifies how the cell's context should be aligned horizontally within the cells in the row; this is shorthand for using align on every cell in the row individually.\u{3}",
        "td\u{1}The td element represents a data cell in a table.\u{1}colspan\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}rowspan\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}headers\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}abbr\u{3}This attribute contains a short abbreviated description of the cell's content. Some user-agents, such as speech readers, may present this description before the content itself.\u{3}\u{2}align\u{3}This enumerated attribute specifies how the cell content's horizontal alignment will be handled. Possible values are: left: The content is aligned to the left of the cell.\u{3}\u{2}axis\u{3}This attribute contains a list of space-separated strings. Each string is the id of a group of cells that this header applies to.\u{3}\u{2}bgcolor\u{3}This attribute defines the background color of each cell in a column. It consists of a 6-digit hexadecimal code as defined in sRGB and is prefixed by '#'.\u{3}",
        "th\u{1}The th element represents a header cell in a table.\u{1}colspan\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}rowspan\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}headers\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}scope\u{3}\u{3}row col rowgroup colgroup\u{2}sorted\u{3}\u{3}\u{2}abbr\u{3}This attribute contains a short abbreviated description of the cell's content. Some user-agents, such as speech readers, may present this description before the content itself.\u{3}\u{2}align\u{3}This enumerated attribute specifies how the cell content's horizontal alignment will be handled. Possible values are: left: The content is aligned to the left of the cell.\u{3}\u{2}axis\u{3}This attribute contains a list of space-separated strings. Each string is the id of a group of cells that this header applies to.\u{3}\u{2}bgcolor\u{3}This attribute defines the background color of each cell in a column. It consists of a 6-digit hexadecimal code as defined in sRGB and is prefixed by '#'.\u{3}",
        "form\u{1}The form element represents a collection of form-associated elements, some of which can represent editable values that can be submitted to a server for processing.\u{1}accept-charset\u{3}A space- or comma-delimited list of character encodings that the server accepts. The browser uses them in the order in which they are listed.\u{3}\u{2}action\u{3}The URI of a program that processes the form information. This value can be overridden by a formaction attribute on a <button> or <input> element.\u{3}\u{2}autocomplete\u{3}Indicates whether input elements can by default have their values automatically completed by the browser.\u{3}on off\u{2}enctype\u{3}When the value of the method attribute is post, enctype is the MIME type of content that is used to submit the form to the server.\u{3}application/x-www-form-urlencoded multipart/form-data text/plain\u{2}method\u{3}The HTTP method that the browser uses to submit the form. Possible values are: post: Corresponds to the HTTP POST method ; form data are included in the body of the form and sent to the server.\u{3}get post dialog\u{2}name\u{3}The name of the form. In HTML 4, its use is deprecated (id should be used instead). It must be unique among the forms in a document and not just an empty string in HTML 5.\u{3}\u{2}novalidate\u{3}This Boolean attribute indicates that the form is not to be validated when submitted. If this attribute is not specified (and therefore the form is validated), this default setting can be overridden...\u{3}\u{2}target\u{3}A name or keyword indicating where to display the response that is received after submitting the form. In HTML 4, this is the name/keyword for a frame.\u{3}_self _blank _parent _top\u{2}accept\u{3}A comma-separated list of content types that the server accepts. Usage note: This attribute has been removed in HTML5 and should no longer be used.\u{3}\u{2}autocapitalize\u{3}This is a nonstandard attribute used by iOS Safari Mobile which controls whether and how the text value for textual form control descendants should be automatically capitalized as it is...\u{3}",
